{"name":"Great Joy","hex":"#d8e6cb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"great","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Taking up a lot of space; large.\n\n Much, more than usual.","Much, more than usual."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Expression of gladness and content about something.","A sarcastic inversion thereof."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/great"},{"term":"joy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A feeling of extreme happiness or cheerfulness, especially related to the acquisition or expectation of something good.","Anything that causes such a feeling."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Expressing appreciation and happiness."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/joy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Joy","blocks":[{"text":"Joy is the state of being that allows one to experience feelings of intense, long-lasting happiness and contentment of life. It is closely related to, and often evoked by, well-being, success, or good fortune. Happiness, pleasure, and gratitude are closely related to joy but are not identical to it."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/A_Smiling_boy_from_Bangladesh.jpg/330px-A_Smiling_boy_from_Bangladesh.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joy","wikidata":"Q935526"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Sara_Ahmed-IMG_6457.jpg/500px-Sara_Ahmed-IMG_6457.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Sara_Ahmed-IMG_6457.jpg/1280px-Sara_Ahmed-IMG_6457.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Sara_Ahmed-IMG_6457.jpg/500px-Sara_Ahmed-IMG_6457.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Scholar S